<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-19.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="061309"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#061309" label="[06:13:09]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I have good news: Lennart agrees to accept patches to bootstrap microhs from hugs only under condition that those patches don't remove pattern guards</span></div><div class="line" id="132020"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#132020" label="[13:20:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: for somebody unfamiliar with haskell, how capable is microhs?</span></div><div class="line" id="132034"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#132034" label="[13:20:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm assuming it's more powerful than hugs but still far behind ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="135649"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#135649" label="[13:56:49]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas your assumption is correct, but that is exactly the reason to bootstrap microhs, as it gives slightly more than 50 ghc-specific extensions</span></div><div class="line" id="140302"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140302" label="[14:03:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">microhs demands only 2 extensions that hugs doesn't have - bang patterns and pattern guards (I was wrong earlier, hugs only has guards, but not pattern guards), Lennart doesn't mind patching out bang patterns, but ideally I would figure out yacc and imperative code and extend hugs myself</span></div><div class="line" id="140427"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140427" label="[14:04:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, it makes sense</span></div><div class="line" id="140442"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140442" label="[14:04:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I just wanted to have a bit more context on where we would be if this works</span></div><div class="line" id="140443"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140443" label="[14:04:43]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in patches that I sent the runtime crash is caused by &quot;get&quot; function from src/MicroHs/StateIO.hs , I had to overplace Debug.Trace to figure that out</span></div><div class="line" id="140622"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#140622" label="[14:06:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also microhs is maintained unlike hugs, so presumably it could support more extensions in the future</span></div><div class="line" id="140649"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140649" label="[14:06:49]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in terms of language I don't know any haskell implementation bigger than microhs (aside from ghc), so this is a big step forward</span></div><div class="line" id="140742"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140742" label="[14:07:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, microhs is actively developed, before I started trying to bootstrap a new language extension got implemented</span></div><div class="line" id="140903"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140903" label="[14:09:03]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however, in terms of base library we are going backward, but eventually Lennart wants microhs to use base from ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="140955"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#140955" label="[14:09:55]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">microhs's base library is incomplete that is</span></div><div class="line" id="141322"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#141322" label="[14:13:22]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also worth mentioning microhs is 20 times slower than ghc and produces 50 times slower binaries than ghc, and it's unlikely to change because microhs relies on combinators virtual machine</span></div><div class="line" id="142318"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#142318" label="[14:23:18]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i notice <a rel="nofollow" href="https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/guix.git/tree/gnu/packages/java-bootstrap.scm#n193">https://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/guix.git/tree/gnu/packages/java-bootstrap.scm#n193</a>  is 429'ing is there a specific .ant.properties / build.xml for ant-bootstrap?</span></div><div class="line" id="144550"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#144550" label="[14:45:50]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ie more specifically: when ant-bootstrap attempts to run it gets com.sun.tools.javac.Main is not on the classpath.</span></div><div class="line" id="145026"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#145026" label="[14:50:26]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it gets it when it's bootstrapping in its 'build' stage (ie after 'check for optional packages')</span></div><div class="line" id="145040"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#145040" label="[14:50:40]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ie after it's read from build.xml</span></div><div class="line" id="145154"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#145154" label="[14:51:54]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas btw, on very positive note microhs supports circular module dependencies, but doesn't use them :)</span></div><div class="line" id="145245"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#145245" label="[14:52:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: are you running it on guix or trying to reproduce it somewhere else?</span></div><div class="line" id="145356"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#145356" label="[14:53:56]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I heard hugs can't support circular module dependencies by design, so supporting those requires major rewrite, which is nobody willing to do</span></div><div class="line" id="145411"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#145411" label="[14:54:11]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">somewhere else</span></div><div class="line" id="145437"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#145437" label="[14:54:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tmg1|michelson: also take a look at <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gitlab.com/stikonas/gentoo-bootstrap/-/blob/master/dev-java/ant-core/ant-core-1.8.4-r1.ebuild?ref_type=heads">https://gitlab.com/stikonas/gentoo-bootstrap/-/blob/master/dev-java/ant-core/ant-core-1.8.4-r1.ebuild?ref_type=heads</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="145440"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#145440" label="[14:54:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this works for me on gentoo</span></div><div class="line" id="145458"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#145458" label="[14:54:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">migth be a bit closer to your distro than guix with its magic...</span></div><div class="line" id="152038"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#152038" label="[15:20:38]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: hrm defining those environment variables actually did seem to get further</span></div><div class="line" id="161247"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#161247" label="[16:12:47]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">actually microhs develops so quickly that the day I started patching is the day conflicting changes were committed, and then another day I submit patches is the day some another conflicting change was committed</span></div><div class="line" id="195456"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#195456" label="[19:54:56]">&lt;tmg1|michelson&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aha figured out why ant isn't building.  gnu classpath 0.93 hangs on File.createTempFile(), always for some reason.</span></div><div class="line" id="201539"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201539" label="[20:15:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">continued bundling packages for i586-tcc-linux-musl.. looking for a few missing utils absent in busybox, blkid etc.</span></div><div class="line" id="201557"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201557" label="[20:15:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's practically impossible to salvage some recent util-linux with linux-2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="201633"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201633" label="[20:16:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">provides an interesting insight into api and abi breakage too</span></div><div class="line" id="201758"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201758" label="[20:17:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least mounting NFS works again, linux-2.4 didn't have FUSE, and it's shfs implementation cannot map uid/gid ownerships</span></div><div class="line" id="201926"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201926" label="[20:19:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">from a practical standpoint, a few pieces shouldn't be missed, when moving back onto linux-2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="202349"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202349" label="[20:23:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the old thinkpad was a real steal, 30Euros and by coincidence it's a perfrect match for linux-2.x, spares me the hazzle to backport ethernet and similar drivers all at once</span></div><div class="line" id="202519"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202519" label="[20:25:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but the linux-2.4 kernel doesn't fully cover all devices yet, booting on some hp t620 thin client, it hasn't got ethernet</span></div><div class="line" id="202603"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202603" label="[20:26:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll probably choose some broadly available usb-dongle to provide some general purpose options for ethernet and wlan, and backport a handfull of drivers for those &quot;only&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="202757"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202757" label="[20:27:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the new years eve hackathon was rather productive nonetheless; can't believe yet myself i succeeded salvage a complete development host with tinycc/linux24/musl</span></div><div class="line" id="202909"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202909" label="[20:29:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a few things are flaky still, since everything was compiled in a cross-development setup</span></div><div class="line" id="203029"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203029" label="[20:30:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i didn't notice any relevant runtime performance degradation with tinycc compiled binaries yet</span></div><div class="line" id="203108"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203108" label="[20:31:08]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you got a single core 1GHz, 1GiB RAM and a little usb flash memory, things should work, easily</span></div><div class="line" id="203639"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203639" label="[20:36:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since it's ~500 packages full review and cleanup takes a little while, since i hacked a few if/else into ebuild scripts to keep the system profile compatible with other cross-targets other than x86/linux24/tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="203828"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203828" label="[20:38:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a hundred dirty little patches and hacks, i try to keep cleanly separated for re-integration, since python/portage cannot be supported with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="203953"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#203953" label="[20:39:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i have to keep an eye on several pacakge trees then: recent portage/upstream, my own tree that is fully forked already and manually re-synced, the bootstrappable tree, and the final system integration that i try to converge with bootstrappable by re-writing packaging</span></div><div class="line" id="222913"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#222913" label="[22:29:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: there are a couple of merge request for you here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pulls">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pulls</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="223558"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#223558" label="[22:35:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thanks</span></div><div class="line" id="224101"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224101" label="[22:41:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">argh, still something is wrong and stage0-posix doesn't work with this M2libc :(</span></div><div class="line" id="224244"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224244" label="[22:42:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh it was probably enum support</span></div><div class="line" id="224737"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224737" label="[22:47:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rkeene: your PR is also not working yet</span></div><div class="line" id="224743"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224743" label="[22:47:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">on aarch64 I'm getting ./AArch64/artifact/hex2_linker-1.M1:569 :Received invalid other; SET_X2_FROM_X1</span></div><div class="line" id="224852"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#224852" label="[22:48:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we need to add &quot;DEFINE SET_X2_FROM_X1 e20301aa&quot; to M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="225044"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#225044" label="[22:50:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess I'll create PR myself...</span></div><div class="line" id="225910"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225910" label="[22:59:10]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">amd64 seems to ran fine in stage0-posix if we only update the M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="230022"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230022" label="[23:00:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/68">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/68</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230034"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230034" label="[23:00:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I'll not grab the latest M2-Planet yet</span></div><div class="line" id="230222"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230222" label="[23:02:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the problem with M2-Planet was that gtker added extern to a file</span></div><div class="line" id="230222"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230222" label="[23:02:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">plus cc_* has a handful of weird C behaviors which can break 100% valid C code</span></div><div class="line" id="230229"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230229" label="[23:02:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah...</span></div><div class="line" id="230242"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230242" label="[23:02:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps just removing extern would work</span></div><div class="line" id="230254"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230254" label="[23:02:54]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">extern is fine if it is in a header file (because we don't pass header files with extern to cc_*)</span></div><div class="line" id="230304"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230304" label="[23:03:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it was not there...</span></div><div class="line" id="230308"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230308" label="[23:03:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it was in cc_types.c</span></div><div class="line" id="230314"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230314" label="[23:03:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so we'll have to fix that</span></div><div class="line" id="230321"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230321" label="[23:03:21]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="230325"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230325" label="[23:03:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it was in this PR <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/60">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/60</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230331"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230331" label="[23:03:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I perhaps merged it too quickly</span></div><div class="line" id="230352"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230352" label="[23:03:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(thogh at least my review found one crash...)</span></div><div class="line" id="230401"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230401" label="[23:04:01]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well enums was another of the C language features needed for M2-Planet to build TCC and I can't blame anyone for wanting it</span></div><div class="line" id="230414"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230414" label="[23:04:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">indeed</span></div><div class="line" id="230434"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230434" label="[23:04:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the code is fairly simple</span></div><div class="line" id="230434"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230434" label="[23:04:34]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">once it and bitslices are done, we are probably only a few hundred lines of C away from M2-Planet being able to directly build TCC</span></div><div class="line" id="230454"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230454" label="[23:04:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though perhaps I'm not 100% happy with how global_token-&gt;s is replaced there</span></div><div class="line" id="230503"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230503" label="[23:05:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe separate variable would have been nicer</span></div><div class="line" id="230506"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230506" label="[23:05:06]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well all of the remaining C features could be done in 2 dozen lines of code or Less</span></div><div class="line" id="230525"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230525" label="[23:05:25]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(each of them)</span></div><div class="line" id="230629"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#230629" label="[23:06:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probaly not pointer arithmetic though</span></div><div class="line" id="230808"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230808" label="[23:08:08]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even pointer arithmetic</span></div><div class="line" id="231021"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231021" label="[23:10:21]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it'll break the universal FUNCTION pointer logic, which means one would have to add *PROPER* C function pointer support (which requires defined arguments to work)</span></div><div class="line" id="231215"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231215" label="[23:12:15]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence why I said a few hundred lines of C away (not impossible just requires *REALLY GOOD* tests and careful work)</span></div><div class="line" id="231354"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231354" label="[23:13:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, with that M2libc patch (<a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/68">https://github.com/oriansj/M2libc/pull/68</a>)  make test-aarch64 works</span></div><div class="line" id="231402"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231402" label="[23:14:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least on my real aarch64 machine</span></div><div class="line" id="231423"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#231423" label="[23:14:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">my userspace qemu is randomly crashing somehow, but I guess that's qemu bug</span></div><div class="line" id="231548"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231548" label="[23:15:48]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wouldn't be the first qemu bug we have found</span></div><div class="line" id="232139"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232139" label="[23:21:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I had some issues with newer qemu versions on riscv64 too</span></div><div class="line" id="232147"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232147" label="[23:21:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(which is why I kept my qemu at 8.0.5)</span></div><div class="line" id="232158"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232158" label="[23:21:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, I do have both aarch64 and riscv64 hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="232231"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232231" label="[23:22:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so I can natively update most of the checksums</span></div><div class="line" id="232236"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232236" label="[23:22:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(except for riscv32)</span></div><div class="line" id="232751"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#232751" label="[23:27:51]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I am limited to x86, AMD64, armv7l and AArch64 native.</span></div><div class="line" id="232835"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#232835" label="[23:28:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I used to have armv7l but not anymore...</span></div><div class="line" id="232930"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#232930" label="[23:29:30]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I hope to revisit the PowerPC port of stage0-posix when I regain enough free time to actually get all the way to cc_* for PowerPC</span></div><div class="line" id="233352"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233352" label="[23:33:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, I think I have a fix for that extern thingy</span></div><div class="line" id="233353"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233353" label="[23:33:53]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another fun feature which would not be a hard add would be is smart locals (no need to worry about variable declarations inside of loops) and it would make the stack clean up at the end of functions just a simple move (which would speed up the resulting binaries and make them smaller)</span></div><div class="line" id="233433"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233433" label="[23:34:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh yeah, that is another thing we a remissing</span></div><div class="line" id="233611"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233611" label="[23:36:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: ok, fix for M2-Planet is here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/61">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/61</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="233647"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233647" label="[23:36:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">In principle I can merge it myself</span></div><div class="line" id="233658"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233658" label="[23:36:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just M2libc that I can't push to</span></div><div class="line" id="233745"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233745" label="[23:37:45]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: I really should give you M2libc permissions</span></div><div class="line" id="233752"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233752" label="[23:37:52]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: too</span></div><div class="line" id="233823"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233823" label="[23:38:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, thanks</span></div><div class="line" id="233836"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233836" label="[23:38:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in principle review from you would be good, but you are often busy these days</span></div><div class="line" id="233845"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#233845" label="[23:38:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but maybe if I do PRs, fossy can then review</span></div><div class="line" id="234001"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234001" label="[23:40:01]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, having a 4 year old who is in the 100 percentile is not easy.</span></div><div class="line" id="234052"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234052" label="[23:40:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, that's understandable</span></div><div class="line" id="234200"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234200" label="[23:42:00]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and honestly I would hate if live-bootstrap's progress ever got stalled on me</span></div><div class="line" id="234259"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234259" label="[23:42:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for some reason I've recently started getting some spam in fossy's name :D</span></div><div class="line" id="234305"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234305" label="[23:43:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">must be because of live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="234324"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#234324" label="[23:43:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some spammers started sending me emails &quot;Hi fosslinux, ...&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="234548"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234548" label="[23:45:48]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lets just hope the crypto bros who send death threats ran out of steam and don't ever come back out of the woodwork. That shit killed soo much of my motivation.</span></div><div class="line" id="235528"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#235528" label="[23:55:28]">&lt;rkeene&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Why would they care about bootstrapping ?</span></div><div class="line" id="235553"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235553" label="[23:55:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rkeene: trusting trust attack</span></div><div class="line" id="235647"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235647" label="[23:56:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not a new thing, it was even a few years ago</span></div><div class="line" id="235652"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235652" label="[23:56:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. see <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2019/08/01/building-bitcoins-software-just-got-a-bit-more-trustless">https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2019/08/01/building-bitcoins-software-just-got-a-bit-more-trustless</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235722"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#235722" label="[23:57:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.reddit.com/r/Bitcoin/comments/smj1ep/bitcoin_v220_and_guix_stronger_defense_against/">https://www.reddit.com/r/Bitcoin/comments/smj1ep/bitcoin_v220_and_guix_stronger_defense_against/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="235837"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#235837" label="[23:58:37]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">The people who care about crypto for better reasons than just scamming have a very direct incentive to produce and use high-assurance software, so there's a subgroup that gets drawn to things like Haskell, Nix, and bootstrapping</span></div><br /></div></body></html>